WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.783
<c.p>[Begin Tape 4558.

2
00:00:00.783 --> 00:00:01.565
Begin Session I.]

3
00:00:01.565 --> 00:00:01.826
<v CHELSEA ARSENEAULT>Alrighty.

4
00:00:01.826 --> 00:00:02.087
<v REBECCA COOPER>Wait.

5
00:00:02.087 --> 00:00:03.652
Can you hang on one second?

6
00:00:03.652 --> 00:00:03.913
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

7
00:00:03.913 --> 00:00:06.000
<v COOPER>I need to just start the camera.

8
00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:06.483
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

9
00:00:06.483 --> 00:00:12.285
Sure, just let me know when you're ready and I can start.

10
00:00:12.285 --> 00:00:12.768
<v COOPER>Okay.

11
00:00:12.768 --> 00:00:15.669
<v ARSENEAULT>Kyle [Tanglao] will cut this out.

12
00:00:15.669 --> 00:00:16.152
Good?

13
00:00:16.152 --> 00:00:16.636
Okay.

14
00:00:16.636 --> 00:00:17.603
All right.

15
00:00:17.603 --> 00:00:20.020
Today is February twelfth, 2016.

16
00:00:20.020 --> 00:00:26.788
I'm Chelsea Arseneault with the [LSU Libraries] T. Harry Williams Center for Oral History.

17
00:00:26.788 --> 00:00:32.589
Today with Mr. Morris Prater in his lovely home in Iowa, Louisiana.

18
00:00:32.589 --> 00:00:46.126
We're here to interview him for his history for the Mossville oral history project that we're doing with . . . in conjunction with the Imperial Calcasieu Museum and Bill Shearman.

19
00:00:46.126 --> 00:00:54.344
So I just want to thank you so much today, Mr. Prater, for agreeing to be interviewed.

20
00:00:54.344 --> 00:00:57.245
We've been looking forward to this

21
00:00:57.245 --> 00:01:02.079
<c.p>interview for, feels like years now, but it’s been months.

22
00:01:02.079 --> 00:01:06.914
Could you state your full name just for the recording?

23
00:01:06.914 --> 00:01:09.331
<v MORRIS PRATER>My name is Morris Prater.

24
00:01:09.331 --> 00:01:09.815
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

25
00:01:09.815 --> 00:01:13.199
And when and where were you born?

26
00:01:13.199 --> 00:01:19.000
<v PRATER>I was born in Mossville in 1929, the twentieth of January.

27
00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:22.805
<v ARSENEAULT>Were you born in your house?

28
00:01:22.805 --> 00:01:23.439
<v PRATER>Yes.

29
00:01:23.439 --> 00:01:24.073
Midwife.

30
00:01:24.073 --> 00:01:26.610
<v ARSENEAULT>There was a midwife?

31
00:01:26.610 --> 00:01:27.244
<v PRATER>Yeah.

32
00:01:27.244 --> 00:01:31.049
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you remember who it was?

33
00:01:31.049 --> 00:01:34.220
<v IRENE PRATER>Probably Ms. [ . . .?] or Aunt Adeline.

34
00:01:34.220 --> 00:01:35.488
<v PRATER>Adeline, probably.

35
00:01:35.488 --> 00:01:37.390
Yeah, probably Adeline.

36
00:01:37.390 --> 00:01:39.293
I don't remember.

37
00:01:39.293 --> 00:01:45.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Did your mom ever talk about your birth?

38
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:46.836
<v PRATER>She did.

39
00:01:46.836 --> 00:01:55.098
You know, I don't remember too much about it.

40
00:01:55.098 --> 00:02:00.607
<v ARSENEAULT>No difficulties or complications or anything?

41
00:02:00.607 --> 00:02:06.115
<v PRATER>No. No, I don't think so.

42
00:02:06.115 --> 00:02:09.787
I did not know.

43
00:02:09.787 --> 00:02:17.131
She did not say, so I don't know.

44
00:02:17.131 --> 00:02:25.393
Back then they didn't tell you too much. [laughs]

45
00:02:25.393 --> 00:02:27.230
<v ARSENEAULT>Very private.

46
00:02:27.230 --> 00:02:28.148
<v PRATER>Yes.

47
00:02:28.148 --> 00:02:39.164
<v ARSENEAULT>But I know a lot of kids were born in the homes.

48
00:02:39.164 --> 00:02:41.000
<v PRATER>Yes.

49
00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:45.500
<v ARSENEAULT>What were your parents' names?

50
00:02:45.500 --> 00:02:51.800
<v PRATER>My mother's name was Eunice Towner Prater.

51
00:02:51.800 --> 00:02:57.200
My dad's name was Erthy Prater.

52
00:02:57.200 --> 00:03:00.800
<v ARSENEAULT>Could you spell Erthy?

53
00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:01.700
<v PRATER>E-R-T-H-Y.

54
00:03:01.700 --> 00:03:07.100
<v ARSENEAULT>Where in Mossville did they live?

55
00:03:07.100 --> 00:03:12.500
<v PRATER>Right off of Prater Road there.

56
00:03:12.500 --> 00:03:20.600
Wasn't on Prater Road, but right off Prater Road.

57
00:03:20.600 --> 00:03:26.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Can you describe the house?

58
00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:40.721
<v PRATER>No. We moved from there before I was old enough to see it . . . remember anything about it.

59
00:03:40.721 --> 00:03:47.649
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you know why they came to Mossville?

60
00:03:47.649 --> 00:03:51.978
<v PRATER>No, I don't know why.

61
00:03:51.978 --> 00:03:55.442
They just moved there.

62
00:03:55.442 --> 00:04:10.163
I don't know why, but they moved from where I was born over on Old Spanish Trail.

63
00:04:10.163 --> 00:04:13.627
That's east of Evergreen.

64
00:04:13.627 --> 00:04:18.822
Had a great big house there.

65
00:04:18.822 --> 00:04:30.080
They rented that and that's where I kind of remember some of that.

66
00:04:30.080 --> 00:04:35.275
It was a real large house.

67
00:04:35.275 --> 00:04:43.069
That's where we stayed there until in the '40s.

68
00:04:43.069 --> 00:04:50.862
And then we moved from there to Prater Road.

69
00:04:50.862 --> 00:05:06.449
So my dad was the first on Prater Road, so that's when they give it the name Prater.

70
00:05:06.449 --> 00:05:08.181
Prater Road.

71
00:05:08.181 --> 00:05:15.975
We were the first on that in the '40s.

72
00:05:15.975 --> 00:05:19.438
Sometime in the '40s.

73
00:05:19.438 --> 00:05:22.902
I don't know when.

74
00:05:22.902 --> 00:05:25.500
And stayed there.

75
00:05:25.500 --> 00:05:33.293
My dad died there, and my mother died there.

76
00:05:33.293 --> 00:05:40.221
And it's about . . . I don't remember what year.

77
00:05:40.221 --> 00:05:56.674
Probably . . . Dad died in the '80s, Mom died in the '90s, but I done forgot the dates on those.

78
00:05:56.674 --> 00:06:06.199
And I started work when I was about sixteen years old.

79
00:06:06.199 --> 00:06:11.395
Got my social security card around

80
00:06:11.395 --> 00:06:13.993
<c.p>sixteen years old.

81
00:06:13.993 --> 00:06:28.714
My first job was we cleared a right of way from [Highway] 90 to [Starks?] . . . Utility line.

82
00:06:28.714 --> 00:06:39.971
My brothers . . . I have . . . Eleven of us children was born all in Mossville.

83
00:06:39.971 --> 00:06:42.569
I'm the oldest.

84
00:06:42.569 --> 00:06:46.899
The baby is Karl Ray.

85
00:06:46.899 --> 00:06:56.424
Me and him have the same birth date, January the twentieth.

86
00:06:56.424 --> 00:07:00.754
I was born in '29.

87
00:07:00.754 --> 00:07:05.083
He was born in '51.

88
00:07:05.083 --> 00:07:12.877
My first job I think I was cutting firewood.

89
00:07:12.877 --> 00:07:18.938
We had two wood yards in Mossville.

90
00:07:18.938 --> 00:07:25.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you know who owned them?

91
00:07:25.000 --> 00:07:27.916
<v PRATER>Two wood yards was in Mossville.

92
00:07:27.916 --> 00:07:30.832
One was . . . The Smith had one.

93
00:07:30.832 --> 00:07:31.318
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

94
00:07:31.318 --> 00:07:34.234
<v PRATER>And Rigmaiden had the other one.

95
00:07:34.234 --> 00:07:41.037
My mother and dad said that we had a store out there, way back.

96
00:07:41.037 --> 00:07:46.383
I don't remember it but it was Moss had his store.

97
00:07:46.383 --> 00:07:50.271
Probably already got all that history from Moss.

98
00:07:50.271 --> 00:07:51.243
<v ARSENEAULT>Pete Moss?

99
00:07:51.243 --> 00:07:51.729
<v PRATER>Pete?

100
00:07:51.729 --> 00:07:53.673
No, not Pete Moss.

101
00:07:53.673 --> 00:07:55.617
It was Jim Moss.

102
00:07:55.617 --> 00:07:56.103
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

103
00:07:56.103 --> 00:07:59.991
<v PRATER>Jim Moss was the daddy of Pete Moss.

104
00:07:59.991 --> 00:08:04.364
Jim Moss had his store there and they said

105
00:08:04.364 --> 00:08:08.738
<c.p>they brought the mail at least once a week.

106
00:08:08.738 --> 00:08:13.112
That was the mail . . . That was the post office.

107
00:08:13.112 --> 00:08:17.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you know where that was located?

108
00:08:17.000 --> 00:08:28.579
<v PRATER>It was located around right there in Mossville, but it was just around across . . . What's the name of the church there, Mama?

109
00:08:28.579 --> 00:08:30.158
Can you remember?

110
00:08:30.158 --> 00:08:31.211
Clover Church?

111
00:08:31.211 --> 00:08:32.263
<v ARSENEAULT>Miracle Deliverance?

112
00:08:32.263 --> 00:08:37.000
<v PRATER>It was right across in front of Clover's Church.

113
00:08:37.000 --> 00:08:38.579
<v ARSENEAULT>Not Mount Zion?

114
00:08:38.579 --> 00:08:42.263
<v PRATER>No, it was east of Mount Zion.

115
00:08:42.263 --> 00:08:53.842
See Mount Zion was all that strip where his Moss was, because Moss the one that give the property for the church.

116
00:08:53.842 --> 00:08:55.421
They say that . . .

117
00:08:55.421 --> 00:08:57.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Jim Moss?

118
00:08:57.000 --> 00:09:01.384
<v PRATER>Jim Moss gave the property for the church.

119
00:09:01.384 --> 00:09:04.123
That's what I was told.

120
00:09:04.123 --> 00:09:07.411
And it was east of that.

121
00:09:07.411 --> 00:09:10.151
East of the church there.

122
00:09:10.151 --> 00:09:15.082
And they brought the mail there once a week.

123
00:09:15.082 --> 00:09:16.726
I don't know.

124
00:09:16.726 --> 00:09:18.370
I don't remember.

125
00:09:18.370 --> 00:09:21.658
I was too small to remember.

126
00:09:21.658 --> 00:09:22.753
Too young.

127
00:09:22.753 --> 00:09:29.329
And after his store went out he had a sugar mill there.

128
00:09:29.329 --> 00:09:33.712
He'd grind cane and cook the syrup there.

129
00:09:33.712 --> 00:09:37.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Did he grow the cane?

130
00:09:37.000 --> 00:09:39.613
<v PRATER>No, he didn't grow the cane.

131
00:09:39.613 --> 00:09:43.097
Everybody just brought cane there from all around.

132
00:09:43.097 --> 00:09:44.839
<v ARSENEAULT>He just processed it.

133
00:09:44.839 --> 00:09:45.710
<v PRATER>He did.

134
00:09:45.710 --> 00:09:50.500
And I used to go down there and sit around there.

135
00:09:50.500 --> 00:09:55.726
I got old enough to run around and watch him do that.

136
00:09:55.726 --> 00:09:58.339
It was something to go do.

137
00:09:58.339 --> 00:09:59.645
We didn't have . . .

138
00:09:59.645 --> 00:10:04.000
<v ARSENEAULT>How did he . . . What . . . I've never seen it done.

139
00:10:04.000 --> 00:10:14.549
<v PRATER>Well he had a . . . it was a little . . . probably a little . . . It was built with a two big drums like.

140
00:10:14.549 --> 00:10:22.460
And the drums turned and he had a . . . well like we call it a [thumb?].

141
00:10:22.460 --> 00:10:32.481
Well anyway, it had a piece come out from it and you put a horse went around with it,

142
00:10:32.481 --> 00:10:36.700
went around and around and that . . . Them drums,

143
00:10:36.700 --> 00:10:45.667
they put the cane in it and the drums squeeze the juice out . . . the cane, you see.

144
00:10:45.667 --> 00:10:49.359
And it would . . . Had a barrel in.

145
00:10:49.359 --> 00:10:55.688
Put a barrel in when you got through with that he would . . .

146
00:10:55.688 --> 00:10:57.270
<v ARSENEAULT>Boil it maybe?

147
00:10:57.270 --> 00:10:59.380
<v PRATER>You would cook it.

148
00:10:59.380 --> 00:11:04.127
Had two big cooking boilers on the inside there.

149
00:11:04.127 --> 00:11:09.928
They was possibly eight by eight or six by six around.

150
00:11:09.928 --> 00:11:12.565
And it was so deep.

151
00:11:12.565 --> 00:11:15.730
And he cooked the syrup, and

152
00:11:15.730 --> 00:11:24.169
<c.p>after he got it cooked they'd come and get it, whoever he was cooking it for.

153
00:11:24.169 --> 00:11:24.696
So . . .

154
00:11:24.696 --> 00:11:26.806
<v ARSENEAULT>Did he charge for . . .?

155
00:11:26.806 --> 00:11:30.498
<v PRATER>I don't really know what happened then.

156
00:11:30.498 --> 00:11:32.080
I couldn't say.

157
00:11:32.080 --> 00:11:35.245
We had a store in Mossville.

158
00:11:35.245 --> 00:11:38.937
That was the Garrett’s had the store.

159
00:11:38.937 --> 00:11:44.738
So back then you could buy as much as you want,

160
00:11:44.738 --> 00:11:53.705
but you got to buy what they want you to have because they packaged it you see.

161
00:11:53.705 --> 00:12:03.726
But then you could buy a dime worth of beans or dollar worth of beans or whatever back then.

162
00:12:03.726 --> 00:12:05.308
But now it's. . .

163
00:12:05.308 --> 00:12:09.000
<v ARSENEAULT>You mean it was like loose?

164
00:12:09.000 --> 00:12:11.175
<v PRATER>Yeah, it was loose.

165
00:12:11.175 --> 00:12:16.612
Everything loose and you'd say you wanted say so much.

166
00:12:16.612 --> 00:12:23.680
And he would weigh up so much and put it in the bad.

167
00:12:23.680 --> 00:12:31.291
So we used to go to the store, you know, and buy like that.

168
00:12:31.291 --> 00:12:39.447
So it . . . Back then it was a whole lot different to what it is now.

169
00:12:39.447 --> 00:12:41.621
It's . . . Everybody loved everybody.

170
00:12:41.621 --> 00:12:44.340
Mossville was a good community.

171
00:12:44.340 --> 00:12:45.427
Real good.

172
00:12:45.427 --> 00:12:49.233
And all the people, you knowed everybody.

173
00:12:49.233 --> 00:12:53.039
Everybody around knowed each one like that.

174
00:12:53.039 --> 00:13:02.825
And if I were somewhere and I did the wrong thing they could whip me and go home,

175
00:13:02.825 --> 00:13:15.874
and you tell your dad or your mom that you got a whipping down at so-and-so's house it was nothing said what you did.

176
00:13:15.874 --> 00:13:17.505
It was fine.

177
00:13:17.505 --> 00:13:19.136
But not now.

178
00:13:19.136 --> 00:13:21.854
It's not like that now.

179
00:13:21.854 --> 00:13:25.117
So it's a whole lot different.

180
00:13:25.117 --> 00:13:28.922
So I can say I was blessed.

181
00:13:28.922 --> 00:13:39.796
I never went to the jailhouse to visit nobody let alone be charged for something so I think I'm blessed.

182
00:13:39.796 --> 00:13:43.602
I think I was raised pretty good.

183
00:13:43.602 --> 00:13:45.777
<v ARSENEAULT>What about your grandparents?

184
00:13:45.777 --> 00:13:52.301
Do you know what their names were and where they were from?

185
00:13:52.301 --> 00:13:52.845
<v PRATER>Yes.

186
00:13:52.845 --> 00:13:56.650
My mother's mother was named Irie . . . Irie . . .

187
00:13:56.650 --> 00:13:57.194
<v ARSENEAULT>Irie?

188
00:13:57.194 --> 00:13:58.282
<v PRATER>Yeah Irie.

189
00:13:58.282 --> 00:13:59.913
Irie Potts Towner.

190
00:13:59.913 --> 00:14:01.000
<v ARSENEAULT>I-R-I-E?

191
00:14:01.000 --> 00:14:04.810
<v PRATER>I think that was it, yes.

192
00:14:04.810 --> 00:14:05.445
<v ARSENEAULT>That's . . .

193
00:14:05.445 --> 00:14:06.716
<v PRATER>Potts Towner.

194
00:14:06.716 --> 00:14:12.431
And my mother's dad name was David Duff Towner.

195
00:14:12.431 --> 00:14:15.607
That's on my mother's side.

196
00:14:15.607 --> 00:14:22.592
On my dad's side my grandmother's name was [Belzoni?] Lyons Prater.

197
00:14:22.592 --> 00:14:27.038
My grandfather's name was Henry [Stafford?] Prater.

198
00:14:27.038 --> 00:14:30.848
<v ARSENEAULT>What do you remember about them?

199
00:14:30.848 --> 00:14:51.806
<v PRATER>Well on my mother's side I learned that they . . . you got some that you can . . . I used to go up there and stay up there lots through the summer and around like that.

200
00:14:51.806 --> 00:14:53.076
My mother's

201
00:14:53.076 --> 00:14:53.711
<c.p>parents.

202
00:14:53.711 --> 00:14:56.251
<v ARSENEAULT>They were in Mossville?

203
00:14:56.251 --> 00:14:56.886
<v PRATER>Yes.

204
00:14:56.886 --> 00:15:10.223
They lived right up . . . It was in Mossville, but it was up the Evergreen [where they work in there now, Evergreen?].

205
00:15:10.223 --> 00:15:21.019
And I used to go up there and my grandfather he planted a truck patch, a garden.

206
00:15:21.019 --> 00:15:33.085
So that was fun to go up there and he planted watermelons and all that, cantaloupes, everything he planted.

207
00:15:33.085 --> 00:15:38.166
So it was fun to go up there.

208
00:15:38.166 --> 00:15:50.232
And I had my uncle was . . . It was teenage boys and all and so I liked to follow them.

209
00:15:50.232 --> 00:15:52.773
So it was good.

210
00:15:52.773 --> 00:15:59.123
On my dad's side it was a whole lot different.

211
00:15:59.123 --> 00:16:01.664
I don't know what.

212
00:16:01.664 --> 00:16:05.474
Grandma, she was kind of strict.

213
00:16:05.474 --> 00:16:10.555
So I just wasn't comfortable with Dad's people.

214
00:16:10.555 --> 00:16:15.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Did they have a garden too?

215
00:16:15.000 --> 00:16:19.628
<v PRATER>No, I don't remember planting nothing there.

216
00:16:19.628 --> 00:16:22.273
I don't remember that.

217
00:16:22.273 --> 00:16:33.512
That was in that area right there where the water well is where they lived right there.

218
00:16:33.512 --> 00:16:35.496
<v ARSENEAULT>Where is that?

219
00:16:35.496 --> 00:16:44.752
<v PRATER>Right there, just west of . . . Well it's right in there where the rec is.

220
00:16:44.752 --> 00:16:50.041
You know where the rec is in Mossville?

221
00:16:50.041 --> 00:16:56.653
Well it's that little patch of woods right in there.

222
00:16:56.653 --> 00:17:00.620
The water well is right there.

223
00:17:00.620 --> 00:17:03.926
Well that's where my dad's . . .

224
00:17:03.926 --> 00:17:07.231
<v ARSENEAULT>Is that called the Brimstone?

225
00:17:07.231 --> 00:17:10.537
Is that the Brimstone area?

226
00:17:10.537 --> 00:17:14.504
<v PRATER>Well the Brimstone is behind there.

227
00:17:14.504 --> 00:17:23.099
Brimstone area, where we used to call the Brimstone, is just behind there.

228
00:17:23.099 --> 00:17:29.711
That's the railroad track like run behind that back there.

229
00:17:29.711 --> 00:17:35.661
But the Brimstone area, that was way out back.

230
00:17:35.661 --> 00:17:45.579
That was probably around Sulphur and sulfur mines, they used to call it I think.

231
00:17:45.579 --> 00:17:50.207
That's salt mine or something out there.

232
00:17:50.207 --> 00:17:54.174
Yes, so it's . . . Mossville was good.

233
00:17:54.174 --> 00:18:07.397
It's good, but it's gone now so all my . . . I was born and raised there so I stayed there too.

234
00:18:07.397 --> 00:18:12.025
I went in the service in 1951.

235
00:18:12.025 --> 00:18:13.347
