That's Clayton there.

482
00:30:08.033 --> 00:30:09.869
<v ARSENEAULT>And he's passed away?

483
00:30:09.869 --> 00:30:11.705
<v PRATER>Yeah, he's under me.

484
00:30:11.705 --> 00:30:14.000
<v ARSENEAULT>And that's his wife?

485
00:30:14.000 --> 00:30:16.636
<v PRATER>Yeah that's his wife there.

486
00:30:16.636 --> 00:30:17.164
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

487
00:30:17.164 --> 00:30:17.691
<v PRATER>Yeah.

488
00:30:17.691 --> 00:30:18.745
So that's . . .

489
00:30:18.745 --> 00:30:20.855
<v ARSENEAULT>That's his brother, Clayton.

490
00:30:20.855 --> 00:30:25.600
<v PRATER>I have one brother I don't have on here.

491
00:30:25.600 --> 00:30:26.655
That's [Bernice?].

492
00:30:26.655 --> 00:30:30.873
It was me, then Clayton, then her. [Bernice?] [ . . .?].

493
00:30:30.873 --> 00:30:31.400
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

494
00:30:31.400 --> 00:30:33.509
Thank you so much.

495
00:30:33.509 --> 00:30:35.091
<v PRATER>Somewhere in there.

496
00:30:35.091 --> 00:30:37.200
I lost them all.

497
00:30:37.200 --> 00:30:43.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What was it like growing up with so many siblings?

498
00:30:43.000 --> 00:30:45.886
<v PRATER>Well it was all right.

499
00:30:45.886 --> 00:30:48.195
It was all right.

500
00:30:48.195 --> 00:30:53.966
Well, Mom was kind of sick back in the '40s.

501
00:30:53.966 --> 00:31:07.819
She went to Alexandria [Louisiana] and stayed for about two and a half months, and I had to take care of all of them.

502
00:31:07.819 --> 00:31:19.362
So I was the oldest so I had to take care of some of them, but not all of them,

503
00:31:19.362 --> 00:31:29.174
because Dad was working so I was the oldest so I had to take care of them.

504
00:31:29.174 --> 00:31:36.678
So I learned how to cook, to wash, iron, keep the house running.

505
00:31:36.678 --> 00:31:46.490
I can cook anything I want to cook now, do a pastry too if I wanted to.

506
00:31:46.490 --> 00:31:53.416
She learnt me the hard way though because she was gone some.

507
00:31:53.416 --> 00:31:55.725
But I enjoyed it.

508
00:31:55.725 --> 00:31:58.611
You know what I mean?

509
00:31:58.611 --> 00:32:05.537
I just . . . I guess forgot about the bad part, the young part.

510
00:32:05.537 --> 00:32:09.000
I just faded it out.

511
00:32:09.000 --> 00:32:12.088
<v ARSENEAULT>Where did you go to school?

512
00:32:12.088 --> 00:32:15.691
<v PRATER>I went to school at Mossville there.

513
00:32:15.691 --> 00:32:23.926
But I didn't get too much schooling because I was out off and on so much.

514
00:32:23.926 --> 00:32:35.765
She was sickly so I . . . Some days she would just stay in bed and I stayed to take care of the little ones.

515
00:32:35.765 --> 00:32:38.338
But I don't regret it.

516
00:32:38.338 --> 00:32:47.603
I made it up to this time so I . . . When I started work I worked right straight through.

517
00:32:47.603 --> 00:32:50.691
So I didn't have any problem.

518
00:32:50.691 --> 00:32:55.838
Did some [little things?]: welding, construction work and all . . .work.

519
00:32:55.838 --> 00:33:00.471
<v ARSENEAULT>You said you were sixteen when you started working?

520
00:33:00.471 --> 00:33:01.500
<v PRATER>About sixteen.

521
00:33:01.500 --> 00:33:03.559
First started cutting wood.

522
00:33:03.559 --> 00:33:08.706
Me and a fellow, Oscar Jordan, we cut wood then.

523
00:33:08.706 --> 00:33:15.912
I believe we got a dollar and a half for a cart of wood.

524
00:33:15.912 --> 00:33:19.000
<v ARSENEAULT>How would you cut it?

525
00:33:19.000 --> 00:33:21.164
<v PRATER>With a cross-cut saw.

526
00:33:21.164 --> 00:33:24.410
Bust it and then stack it.

527
00:33:24.410 --> 00:33:27.656
Four high and eight foot long.

528
00:33:27.656 --> 00:33:33.607
We had to saw it, then bust it, then stack it.

529
00:33:33.607 --> 00:33:37.934
And we get a dollar and fifty cents.

530
00:33:37.934 --> 00:33:41.180
<v ARSENEAULT>You thought you were rich, huh?

531
00:33:41.180 --> 00:33:42.262
<v PRATER>We did.

532
00:33:42.262 --> 00:33:43.885
We did that.

533
00:33:43.885 --> 00:33:57.951
And then my next job was clearing up . . . we cleared the right of way from [Highway] 90 to Starks for a utility line to go through.

534
00:33:57.951 --> 00:34:01.197
Then I went to Maplewood [Louisiana].

535
00:34:01.197 --> 00:34:04.443
Went to Maplewood to cut grass.

536
00:34:04.443 --> 00:34:14.721
Then cut grass for a while, then [formed a?] movement from the sewage disposal plant and I worked there.

537
00:34:14.721 --> 00:34:20.131
I was working there when I went in the service.

538
00:34:20.131 --> 00:34:25.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Did you get drafted or did you enlist?

539
00:34:25.000 --> 00:34:27.076
<v PRATER>No, I was drafted.

540
00:34:27.076 --> 00:34:28.634
I was drafted.

541
00:34:28.634 --> 00:34:32.786
And see that Maplewood was a government job.

542
00:34:32.786 --> 00:34:35.382
I mean governor housing project.

543
00:34:35.382 --> 00:34:40.053
See they built that when the airport come in.

544
00:34:40.053 --> 00:34:47.840
See they have a lot of soldiers here in Lake Charles back in the '40s.

545
00:34:47.840 --> 00:34:51.992
<v ARSENEAULT>Was there a training base there or something?

546
00:34:51.992 --> 00:34:59.779
<v PRATER>Yes, it was a training base set up and planes coming in and out there.

547
00:34:59.779 --> 00:35:02.374
That's a big airport there.

548
00:35:02.374 --> 00:35:10.679
It can take any plane that they got now on that airport there in Lake Charles.

549
00:35:10.679 --> 00:35:13.275
I went to work there.

550
00:35:13.275 --> 00:35:17.947
So a lot of soldiers moved there in Maplewood.

551
00:35:17.947 --> 00:35:23.137
That's where a whole lot of soldiers moved in Maplewood.

552
00:35:23.137 --> 00:35:28.328
I was working there when I went in the service.

553
00:35:28.328 --> 00:35:29.885
So the superintendent. . . .

554
00:35:29.885 --> 00:35:33.000
<v ARSENEAULT>You got drafted in 1951?

555
00:35:33.000 --> 00:35:34.048
<v PRATER>1951, yes.

556
00:35:34.048 --> 00:35:36.667
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you remember the day?

557
00:35:36.667 --> 00:35:38.762
<v PRATER>Yes, January the eleventh.

558
00:35:38.762 --> 00:35:42.952
<v ARSENEAULT>Like do you remember what happened that day?

559
00:35:42.952 --> 00:35:45.571
Like how you were feeling.

560
00:35:45.571 --> 00:35:52.905
<v PRATER>I was feeling rough that day. [laughs] January the eleventh I went to Alexandria.

561
00:35:52.905 --> 00:36:01.286
Come back and eight to ten days I got my notice to come back to Alexandria.

562
00:36:01.286 --> 00:36:04.429
Bring enough clothes for three days.

563
00:36:04.429 --> 00:36:04.952
<v ARSENEAULT>Processing?

564
00:36:04.952 --> 00:36:06.000
<v PRATER>I know.

565
00:36:06.000 --> 00:36:07.571
I left then.

566
00:36:07.571 --> 00:36:14.381
I didn't come back to [ . . .?] my training and then I got a leave.

567
00:36:14.381 --> 00:36:17.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Where was boot camp?

568
00:36:17.000 --> 00:36:18.177
<v PRATER>In Virginia.

569
00:36:18.177 --> 00:36:20.531
Right of Washington, D.C.

570
00:36:20.531 --> 00:36:24.063
<v ARSENEAULT>What was the . . . Was it Fort . . .

571
00:36:24.063 --> 00:36:25.240
<v PRATER>Fort [Berrymoore?].

572
00:36:25.240 --> 00:36:28.771
<v ARSENEAULT>What do you remember about training?

573
00:36:28.771 --> 00:36:29.360
<v PRATER>Well . . .

574
00:36:29.360 --> 00:36:31.714
<v ARSENEAULT>Was it segregated then?

575
00:36:31.714 --> 00:36:34.069
<v PRATER>No, it was distorting.

576
00:36:34.069 --> 00:36:44.663
Not all the N.C.O.'s some of them were white, but all the troops in there was all colored.

577
00:36:44.663 --> 00:36:57.023
One spot they had all the white in a different spot, but it started right when I got out in '53.

578
00:36:57.023 --> 00:37:01.731
It was kind of mixing in in '53.

579
00:37:01.731 --> 00:37:10.560
But I don't know I didn't get in to any trouble and I got passes.

580
00:37:10.560 --> 00:37:15.269
Got pretty good pass for a standing inspection.

581
00:37:15.269 --> 00:37:17.034
My shoes shining.

582
00:37:17.034 --> 00:37:19.389
So I enjoyed it.

583
00:37:19.389 --> 00:37:25.863
When I could come home and see her I enjoyed that.

584
00:37:25.863 --> 00:37:28.217
So it was nice.

585
00:37:28.217 --> 00:37:31.160
I had a good life.

586
00:37:31.160 --> 00:37:36.457
In the service I didn't get into any trouble.

587
00:37:36.457 --> 00:37:43.520
So I . . . But I got out when my two years was up.

588
00:37:43.520 --> 00:37:46.463
I didn't re-up no more.

589
00:37:46.463 --> 00:37:51.760
But I was in reserve though for six years.

590
00:37:51.760 --> 00:37:55.291
<v ARSENEAULT>What was your . . . Was it M.O.S.?

591
00:37:55.291 --> 00:38:00.000
What was your job in the military?

592
00:38:00.000 --> 00:38:03.750
<v PRATER>I was a truck driver.

593
00:38:03.750 --> 00:38:07.500
See I was in headquarters.

594
00:38:07.500 --> 00:38:09.750
In headquarters Fort [?].

595
00:38:09.750 --> 00:38:17.250
Headquarters would take care of all the rest of the [ . . .?].

596
00:38:17.250 --> 00:38:19.500
It was good.

597
00:38:19.500 --> 00:38:25.500
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you remember any of your superior officers?

598
00:38:25.500 --> 00:38:30.750
<v PRATER>I done about forgot some of them.

599
00:38:30.750 --> 00:38:42.000
Let’s see, I had . . . Let’s see, the captain was . . . We gave the nickname Cooter Brown.

600
00:38:42.000 --> 00:38:45.000
It wasn't his name.

601
00:38:45.000 --> 00:38:46.500
<v ARSENEAULT>Colonel Brown?

602
00:38:46.500 --> 00:38:48.000
<v PRATER>Cooter Brown.

603
00:38:48.000 --> 00:38:49.500
<v ARSENEAULT>Cooter Brown.

604
00:38:49.500 --> 00:38:54.000
Why'd y'all call him that?

605
00:38:54.000 --> 00:38:59.176
<v PRATER>Because he was kind of mean. [laughs] Yes, he was kind of mean.

606
00:38:59.176 --> 00:38:59.972
Let’s see.

607
00:38:59.972 --> 00:39:01.565
What was his name?

608
00:39:01.565 --> 00:39:03.556
I done forgot his name.

609
00:39:03.556 --> 00:39:04.352
I forgot.

610
00:39:04.352 --> 00:39:06.343
I can't think his name.

611
00:39:06.343 --> 00:39:08.731
<v IRENE PRATER>[ . . . ?] I can't think his name now.

612
00:39:08.731 --> 00:39:09.926
I had it.

613
00:39:09.926 --> 00:39:11.917
<v PRATER>We had a lieutenant there.

614
00:39:11.917 --> 00:39:13.111
<v ARSENEAULT>He was mean?

615
00:39:13.111 --> 00:39:15.500
<v PRATER>I can't think of his name.

616
00:39:15.500 --> 00:39:17.889
I can't think of their names.

617
00:39:17.889 --> 00:39:21.074
That's what I said sometimes I just forget.

618
00:39:21.074 --> 00:39:23.065
I can't think of them.

619
00:39:23.065 --> 00:39:29.435
The name I know just as good as I do mine but I just . . . It's gone.

620
00:39:29.435 --> 00:39:33.417
<v ARSENEAULT>It will come back to you probably in a second.

621
00:39:33.417 --> 00:39:37.000
But he was . . . Was he just grouchy or . . .?

622
00:39:37.000 --> 00:39:44.080
<v PRATER>Well he wasn't really grouchy but you just . . . It was that he was strict.

623
00:39:44.080 --> 00:39:46.608
I'll put it that way.

624
00:39:46.608 --> 00:39:48.125
He was strict.

625
00:39:48.125 --> 00:39:48.631
So . . .

626
00:39:48.631 --> 00:39:49.642
<v IRENE PRATER>Collin Powell.

627
00:39:49.642 --> 00:39:50.148
<v PRATER>Yeah.

628
00:39:50.148 --> 00:39:51.159
Collin Powell.

629
00:39:51.159 --> 00:39:55.205
That was the captain over the division there.

630
00:39:55.205 --> 00:40:00.767
So that was him, but I can't think of the lieutenant.

631
00:40:00.767 --> 00:40:02.790
I had a lieutenant.

632
00:40:02.790 --> 00:40:08.352
First lieutenant, second lieutenant, but I can't think of their names.

633
00:40:08.352 --> 00:40:13.409
It was the engineer construction battalion what I was in.

634
00:40:13.409 --> 00:40:22.511
So if I’d have went overseas we'd have been the first to go in because we'd built roads,

635
00:40:22.511 --> 00:40:27.568
bridges, and stuff like that for everything to go in.

636
00:40:27.568 --> 00:40:29.591
But I didn't go.

637
00:40:29.591 --> 00:40:35.153
Some of my outfit went over because they [ . . .?] and went over.

638
00:40:35.153 --> 00:40:45.773
When we were scheduled to go over I didn't have enough time left, so they just transferred me to another outfit.

639
00:40:45.773 --> 00:40:48.807
And then the ones went over.

640
00:40:48.807 --> 00:40:50.324
They went overseas.

641
00:40:50.324 --> 00:40:53.864
<v ARSENEAULT>Did any of your buddies go overseas?

642
00:40:53.864 --> 00:40:57.909
<v PRATER>Not . . . No, not the ones from Lake Charles.

643
00:40:57.909 --> 00:41:02.966
But had some from Houston [Texas] went over and Alexandria.

644
00:41:02.966 --> 00:41:05.494
Different places went over, yeah.

645
00:41:05.494 --> 00:41:08.528
But we went through training though.

646
00:41:08.528 --> 00:41:13.585
We took over through what they call the infiltration course.

647
00:41:13.585 --> 00:41:20.665
Set up machine guns out there, out front there line them up, machine guns.

648
00:41:20.665 --> 00:41:25.722
And we come in on the other end. [ . . .?] come in.

649
00:41:25.722 --> 00:41:37.352
And they had it was in the summertime dry but they put water out there I guess because it was muddy and wet.

650
00:41:37.352 --> 00:41:39.375
They had poles dug.

651
00:41:39.375 --> 00:41:43.926
They had done them right in the holes . . . charges.

652
00:41:43.926 --> 00:41:52.017
And they had barbed wire stretched all in there and they had logs all in there.

653
00:41:52.017 --> 00:41:55.557
The machine guns set up over here.

654
00:41:55.557 --> 00:41:58.085
They was shooting live ammo.

655
00:41:58.085 --> 00:42:01.625
They told us before we go in.

656
00:42:01.625 --> 00:42:03.142
Say, "Stay down.

657
00:42:03.142 --> 00:42:07.693
If you get up it cut you in two."

658
00:42:07.693 --> 00:42:10.222
They said, "This is it.

659
00:42:10.222 --> 00:42:14.773
We going overseas and you got to be ready."

660
00:42:14.773 --> 00:42:15.784
So Okay.

661
00:42:15.784 --> 00:42:18.818
We went in in the daytime.

662
00:42:18.818 --> 00:42:26.909
We went back in at night and that night you could feel the heat over you.

663
00:42:26.909 --> 00:42:31.966
They were shooting I guess about that high over us.

664
00:42:31.966 --> 00:42:35.000
<v ARSENEAULT>The heat of the bullets?

665
00:42:35.000 --> 00:42:35.468
<v PRATER>Yeah.

666
00:42:35.468 --> 00:42:37.338
And they were live.

667
00:42:37.338 --> 00:42:38.740
It was real.

668
00:42:38.740 --> 00:42:43.416
And they told us, "As you get up that's it."

669
00:42:43.416 --> 00:42:47.623
We went through with our packs on our back.

670
00:42:47.623 --> 00:42:49.026
Whole full-filled pack.

671
00:42:49.026 --> 00:42:50.429
And your rifle.

672
00:42:50.429 --> 00:43:01.649
And when you get through then your rifle had to be clean because you had to have it ready so you could fight . . . shoot.

673
00:43:01.649 --> 00:43:10.065
Come back in that night and at night it wasn't nothing but a streak of fire over us.

674
00:43:10.065 --> 00:43:12.403
Far as that ammo went.

675
00:43:12.403 --> 00:43:21.753
Every fifth round was a tracer and it was shooting so fast it wasn't nothing but a streak of fire.

676
00:43:21.753 --> 00:43:26.896
Gave one of them a steak of fire right over you.

677
00:43:26.896 --> 00:43:29.701
So that was the getting ready.

678
00:43:29.701 --> 00:43:36.247
That's the only time that I felt bad about it because it was real.

679
00:43:36.247 --> 00:43:44.662
One boy got his eyes . . . I don't know if it put his eye out or not but anyway.

680
00:43:44.662 --> 00:43:54.013
He crawled in where a charge was of dynamite and when it went up well I was close to him.

681
00:43:54.013 --> 00:43:57.753
It went up just water and mud come

682
00:43:57.753 --> 00:43:58.221
<c.p>down.

683
00:43:58.221 --> 00:44:00.091
Down on your head.

684
00:44:00.091 --> 00:44:01.961
And it was something.

685
00:44:01.961 --> 00:44:06.169
Well I don't know, but I got to go.

686
00:44:06.169 --> 00:44:07.571
It's after two.

687
00:44:07.571 --> 00:44:09.442
<v ARSENEAULT>It's about that time.

688
00:44:09.442 --> 00:44:10.377
<v PRATER>Yes ma'am.

689
00:44:10.377 --> 00:44:11.312
It's after.

690
00:44:11.312 --> 00:44:12.247
I got . . .

691
00:44:12.247 --> 00:44:15.052
<v ARSENEAULT>Well we have so much more . . .

692
00:44:15.052 --> 00:44:23.000
<v PRATER>I have to pick up my . . . What it is I have to pick up my grandson.

693
00:44:23.000 --> 00:44:23.342
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

694
00:44:23.342 --> 00:44:24.027
<v PRATER>From school.

695
00:44:24.027 --> 00:44:24.370
Yeah.

696
00:44:24.370 --> 00:44:25.055
<v ARSENEAULT>Yes well . . .

697
00:44:25.055 --> 00:44:26.767
<v PRATER>You can get the pictures.

698
00:44:26.767 --> 00:44:29.849
Mama you can show them the pictures and all.

699
00:44:29.849 --> 00:44:32.247
<v ARSENEAULT>Yes, we got pictures of these and . . .

700
00:44:32.247 --> 00:44:33.959
<v PRATER>Oh you one got them?

701
00:44:33.959 --> 00:44:36.356
<v ARSENEAULT>She took a picture of the picture.

702
00:44:36.356 --> 00:44:36.699
<v PRATER>Okay.

703
00:44:36.699 --> 00:44:37.041
Alright.

704
00:44:37.041 --> 00:44:37.726
<v ARSENEAULT>Yes sir.

705
00:44:37.726 --> 00:44:42.521
And we'll definitely come back and get the rest of this story next time.

706
00:44:42.521 --> 00:44:42.863
<v PRATER>Okay.

707
00:44:42.863 --> 00:44:43.890
<v ARSENEAULT>If that's alright.

708
00:44:43.890 --> 00:44:46.288
<v PRATER>Well, if I can think of it.

709
00:44:46.288 --> 00:44:46.973
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh yeah.

710
00:44:46.973 --> 00:44:48.000
You'll think . . .

711
00:44:48.000 --> 00:44:48.429
<c.p>[End Tape 4558.

712
00:44:48.429 --> 00:44:48.857
End Session I.]
